WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.040
Let u be odd and greater than one, and
suppose f times f star equals u squared.

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:10.620
Let I contain the distinct primes dividing
u. Our evaluated values belong to B,

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:14.980
the integers with i and the chosen
prime roots of unity adjoined.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:17.600
Fix one prime p in I.

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:22.400
Pair each subset S not containing p
with the subset obtained by adding p.

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:25.880
The same alternating product
becomes a product of paired ratios,

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:29.500
raised to alternating positive
and negative first powers.

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:34.480
For two distinct primes, p and
q, move the four evaluations into

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:38.360
two ratios in the p direction.
The second ratio is inverted.

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:42.380
Every factor keeps its original
numerator or denominator role.

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:45.980
We are regrouping one
fixed number, Delta of f.

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:49.460
At every maximal ideal above p,

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:54.620
the preceding local comparison makes each
paired ratio a unit with residue one.

00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:57.620
A unit has an inverse
in the same local ring.

00:00:58.060 --> 00:01:02.940
Products and inverses preserve both
being a unit and having residue one.

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:05.920
Thus Delta has both properties there.

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:11.220
Change the pairing direction to q. The
grouping changes, but Delta does not.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:17.100
Repeat for every prime dividing u,
and at every maximal ideal above that

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:21.020
prime. Keep this residue-one information
for the later restriction on the order.

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:26.100
Now consider a maximal ideal containing
none of the primes dividing u.

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:29.520
In this local ring, u
squared is invertible.

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:33.680
The evaluated norm identity gives
an explicit inverse for each value:

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:36.640
its complex conjugate
divided by u squared.

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:40.200
Multiplying the value by
this inverse gives one.

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:43.560
Every evaluated factor is
therefore a unit here as well,

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:47.400
so the alternating product has no
denominator obstruction. Together,

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:51.520
the two cases put Delta in B
localized at every maximal ideal.

00:01:52.940 --> 00:01:55.680
Why do the local checks
give a global element of B?

00:01:56.360 --> 00:02:00.060
Write z for Delta, and
suppose z is outside B.

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:05.660
Form the ideal J of all elements b in
B for which b times z belongs to B.

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:10.560
This ideal is proper: if it contained
one, z would already be in B.

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:13.280
Choose a maximal ideal containing J.

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:17.440
Local membership writes
z as a divided by s,

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:21.620
with a and s in B and s
outside that maximal ideal.

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:26.000
But s times z equals a.
Therefore s clears z,

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:31.080
so s belongs to J, inside the very
ideal it was required to avoid.

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:34.320
This contradiction proves
Delta belongs to B.

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:39.960
The generators of B are roots of
unity, hence algebraic integers.

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:44.860
Sums and products of algebraic
integers remain algebraic integers,

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:50.360
so Delta is an algebraic integer.
Separately, we already proved that all its

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:54.500
conjugates have magnitude one. These are
the two inputs for Kronecker's theorem.

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:59.400
Keep the local residue-one result too;
the order restriction still comes later.
